---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Content guidelines"
  description="Our safe-messaging and respectful-language standards for educational mental health writing."
>
  <div class="container narrow section">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Care in the words we choose</p><h1>
        Writing with people in mind.
      </h1><p>
        These guidelines set boundaries for articles, media descriptions,
        titles, and illustrations.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Write for a person, not a diagnosis</h2>
      <p>
        Use plain language and explain unfamiliar terms. Do not diagnose
        readers, label a person by a condition, or imply that one article
        explains their experience. Avoid blaming language and assumptions about
        someone's background, family, faith, gender, or access to care.
      </p>
      <h2>Discuss distress without harmful detail</h2>
      <p>
        Do not include methods, instructions, comparisons, graphic descriptions,
        or details that could enable self-harm or suicide. Do not romanticize or
        sensationalize suicide, self-harm, abuse, or trauma. Avoid images and
        headlines that turn distress into spectacle.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use measured language. Avoid presenting a death or an act of self-harm
        as inevitable, a solution, a single-cause event, or an outcome that
        gives someone status or attention. Focus on support, the person's wider
        life, and the possibility of getting help without promising recovery on
        a schedule.
      </p>
      <h2>Give readers a choice and a route to support</h2>
      <p>
        Mark sensitive articles in the content data. Show a short support notice
        and a direct link to <a href="/get-help/">Get Help</a>. Keep page titles
        neutral. Do not autoplay media, play sound on load, show pop-ups, or use
        alarming animation. Readers should be able to leave or skip a topic
        without pressure.
      </p>
      <h2>Keep claims within the evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Never invent studies, statistics, qualifications, professional review,
        or personal stories. Link to sources that support factual claims,
        describe uncertainty, and separate general information from individual
        advice. Do not promote unproven treatments, supplements, or “cures.”
      </p>
      <h2>Keep commercial interests clear</h2>
      <p>
        Disclose any permitted affiliate link or sponsorship. Do not place ads
        or affiliate recommendations on Get Help or articles marked sensitive.
        Commercial partners must not shape health claims or be presented as
        independent clinical evidence.
      </p>
      <h2>Review before publishing</h2>
      <p>
        A human editor needs to check wording, references, sensitive material,
        and context before a sample becomes a published article. Record only
        reviews that actually happened. The site owner still needs to confirm
        the real editorial workflow and correction contact.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> for the current
        process and its limitations. The <a href="/disclaimer/"
          >medical disclaimer</a
        > explains what educational reading can and cannot offer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
